<!--Post: 4060--><!--Time: 1105601010-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4060"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 11:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Luvmysteries - there was a fair lapse of time between crime and receipt of the poem - perhaps enough for there to be something in the paper.  In any case, BTK had enough time in the house to do some research on the occupants.  I don&#39;t think that BTK would care if his evil joke poetry would go to the victim or the mother.  If the poem is indeed related to the military school ode to &#34;Ana-lytical&#34;, then it may have just been convenience of the joke, even if his target was the daughter.  BTK&#39;s dark humor seems fairly unconstrained. <BR> <BR>Back to the thread &#40;Tom&#39;s a stickler on this&#41;, I don&#39;t know if the Anna letter sheds light on the first letter or not.  Despite what is said elsewhere, I&#39;m not sure everybody agrees on who the target was, much less when it was decided, so no help on when victims are identified. <BR> <BR>Personally, I believe that most of what he says is lies, and that we should not use his statements - even his pleas in the first letter - as sources of hard information, particularly about real MO. <BR> <BR>The extreme savagery of the attacks, combined with a variety of other data, lead me to this hypothesis &#40;this idea is totally off the wall - just my gut - so don&#39;t ask me for any real proof&#41;. <BR> <BR>I think these murders are planned in advance, with knowledge of the victim, because I believe they are vengeance-based.  Not vengeance for anything that most people would regard as normal.  Things on the order of &#34;I&#39;m sorry - I&#39;ve already got a boyfriend.&#34;  I think BTK is completely unable to handle rejection of any kind.  On the surface, he&#39;s like the nebbish in &#34;Office Space&#34; who lets everybody walk all over him, and mumbles to himself as people unknowingly abuse and ignore him.  But underneath, his rage is excruciating, and is simply never released.  And the key is that he got his sex and revenge wires crossed during his dysfunctional adolescence. <BR> <BR>I think each of the victims slighted BTK at some point in his life.  I don&#39;t know when it was, but I think it could have been something so minor that most people wouldn&#39;t think twice about it.  Turned down for a date.  Wouldn&#39;t dance with him at the one school dance he went to.  Rolled eyes.  Giggles, gossip, and pointing at work.  Down underneath, he never got past his first brush-off.  Whatever it is, he goes postal when it happens.  But instead of coming back to the office with an AK-47, he does some sexual rumination for a while, and then comes back with his bowling bag. <BR> <BR>So according to my theory, his target is his rage, which knows no boundary once it is incited by somebody.  Killing the whole family is more vengeful than just the person who slighted him, so why not?  The only question is what order.  Maybe Anna gave him a dirty look at Dillon&#39;s.  Maybe the daughter wouldn&#39;t go out with him.  After either, they&#39;re both targets.  Was it Mr. or Mrs. Otero?  Once his fantasy begins, it doesn&#39;t matter. <BR> <BR>I guess I got a little long-winded, there.  Hope I didn&#39;t bore you to tears.  Thanks!  -pax<!--/Text--></td></tr>
